
Password Reset Does Not Log Out Existing Sessions, Allowing Persistent Unauthorized Access

#######################################
original written by : Zabed Ullah Poyel
#######################################
Summary

SingleStore Portal fails to invalidate previously authenticated sessions after a password reset.
Even after the user resets their password (typically done due to suspected compromise), any existing session tokens remain fully valid in other browsers or devices.

This creates a situation where an attacker with an existing session can bypass the entire password reset security mechanism and continue accessing the account.
This behavior violates industry-standard security practices and aligns with CWE-613: Insufficient Session Expiration.

Steps to Reproduce

Target: https://portal.singlestore.com
Test Account (for demonstration):

    Email: 56p8zjppoy@jkotypc.com
    Original Password: ?k2H6s710r
    New Password After Reset: ?k2H6s72610r

Reproduction Flow:

    Open Browser A

    Login using original password.
    Confirm successful authentication.

2. Open Browser B

    Login using the same account and same original password.
    Confirm successful authentication here as well.

3. In Browser A:

    Logout.
    Initiate “Forgot Password” → Reset the password.
    Login again using the new password.

4. Switch to Browser B:

    Refresh the page or browse authenticated content.
    Browser B remains logged in, despite the password reset.

Observed Result

Old session tokens → NOT invalidated
Browser B → Still authenticated
Attacker with any active session → Retains full access
Expected Result

All existing sessions should be immediately logged out upon password reset.
Any request made with an old session token should be rejected and require fresh authentication.

