Impact Analysis
Confidentiality

An attacker with a stolen session or access to a previously logged-in device still retains full access even after the victim resets the password. Sensitive data remains exposed.
Integrity

The attacker can perform authorized actions (depending on privileges):

    Modify cluster configurations
    Execute database operations
    Change settings
    Access billing or organizational data

User Safety & Trust

Password reset is expected to revoke access for unauthorized users.
Failure to enforce this breaks user trust and reduces the security value of the reset process.
Severity

    High for regular accounts with sensitive data
    Critical for admin, database managers, or billing roles

CWE Mapping

    CWE-613 — Insufficient Session Expiration
    Related:
    CWE-384 — Session Fixation (conceptual relation)
    CWE-522 — Insufficiently Protected Credentials (contextual)

Why This Bug Matters

Session invalidation after a password reset is a foundational security expectation.
Users rely on it for protection after:

    Device loss
    Compromised accounts
    Shared computer usage
    Suspicious login activity

Failing to invalidate sessions effectively nullifies the entire password reset security mechanism, putting users at risk.
